course: everyday
lesson: 239
title: Litter and caring for nature
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The rule of the outdoors is «Leave no trace»: leave a place as you found it, or better. Take your rubbish home or to a bin, even an apple core, which takes months to rot and teaches animals to come close to people.
explain: Litter does real harm. Animals swallow plastic and balloon pieces or get tangled in strings, so never let a balloon go outdoors.
explain: Nature is also people's and animals' home. Rocks in a stream shelter insects and fish, quiet lets animals feel safe, and wild frogs and lizards usually die if taken home.
explain: If you see someone harming an animal or starting a fire, do not argue with them; tell a grown-up, a ranger (a person whose job is to look after a park or reserve) or the police, who can stop it safely.
explain: Example: a girl in Colorado finishes her granola bar on a trail. There is no bin, so she folds the wrapper into her pocket and drops it in the bin at the car park.
fact: Keep a snack wrapper in your pocket or bag until you find a bin, because litter harms animals and spoils places for everyone.
fact: You can pick up safe litter left by others with gloves or a litter picker; it helps nature and sets a good example.
fact: Take apple cores and banana peels home or put them in a bin; they take months to rot and teach animals to come close to people.
fact: Never release balloons outdoors, because they come down as litter that animals swallow or get tangled in.
fact: Do not draw on rocks, trees or signs, because it spoils the place for others and is hard to remove.
fact: Leave rocks in a stream where they are, because they are homes for insects and fish.
fact: Speak quietly in nature and leave music at home, because noise frightens animals and disturbs other visitors.
fact: Look at wild frogs, lizards and tadpoles but leave them in their home; they usually die in captivity and some are protected.
fact: In a nature reserve keep a dog on a lead and pick up its poo, because dogs chase wildlife and disturb ground-nesting birds.
fact: If you see someone harm an animal or start a fire, do not argue; tell a grown-up, a ranger or the police.
exercise: You finish a snack on a walk and there is no bin. What do you do? | Keep the wrapper in your bag until you find a bin | Hide it under a big rock, where no one will ever see it | Drop it, because the cleaners will come and pick it up | Bury it deep in the soil by the side of the path so it rots
exercise: You see an old can lying by the path. What can you do? | Pick it up with gloves or a litter picker and bin it | Kick it further off the path, where nobody can see it | Leave it, because you did not drop it | Throw it into the stream so the water washes it away
exercise: Is it fine to throw an apple core into the bushes? | No, it takes months to rot and draws animals to people | Yes, fruit is natural and disappears in a day or so | Yes, it feeds the wild animals a good, healthy meal | Yes, as long as nobody can see it
exercise: Why should you not let a helium balloon go at a party in the park? | It comes down as litter that animals swallow | It flies right up into space and pops without any harm | It will be found by another child who will keep it | It turns into rain once it gets high up in the clouds
exercise: A friend wants to paint his name on a big rock in the park. What do you say? | No, it spoils the place and is hard to remove | Yes, as long as he uses a nice bright colour | Yes, rocks in parks are not anyone's property | Only if he paints it small
exercise: Why leave stones in a stream where they are? | Insects and fish live under them | Moving stones makes the water rise | The stones are too heavy to carry | Stones in water are full of germs
exercise: Your cousin wants to play loud music on a forest walk. What do you suggest? | Leave it off; noise frightens animals and bothers visitors | Turn it up loud so the animals in the trees can enjoy it too | Play it only near the birds' nests | It is fine, because forests are big
exercise: You find tadpoles in a pond and want to keep them in a jar. What is best? | Look at them and leave them in the pond | Take them all home in a jar of tap water | Take just a few home and keep them forever | Move them to a bigger puddle on the road
exercise: Why should a dog be on a lead in a nature reserve? | Dogs chase wildlife and disturb ground-nesting birds | Dogs get bored and sleepy if they run around too much | Leads keep the dog's fur clean | Dogs are not allowed to walk on grass in any nature reserve
exercise: What else should you do when walking a dog in a reserve? | Pick up its poo and take it to a bin | Let it dig for rabbits wherever it likes | Let it drink from every pond it passes | Let it bark loudly to scare away the foxes
exercise: You see an older boy throwing stones at ducks. What should you do? | Tell a grown-up, a ranger or the police | Shout at him and push him away from the pond | Throw stones back at him so he knows how it feels | Join in so that he does not pick on you as well
exercise: You see someone lighting a fire in dry woods where fires are banned. What do you do? | Do not argue; tell a grown-up or a ranger at once | Run over and stamp on the fire yourself until it goes out | Shout at them until they stop | Stay and watch for a while to see if the fire spreads
exercise: What does «Leave no trace» mean? | Leave a place as you found it, or better | Walk on tiptoe so you leave no footprints | Never go to the same place twice | Take a souvenir from every place you visit
test: A boy in Wales eats a banana on the beach. Where should the peel go? | In a bin or back home in his bag | Into the sand, where it will rot overnight | Into the sea for the fish to eat | Under a rock away from the path
test: Your school wants to release 100 balloons for its birthday. What would be kinder to nature? | Something else, like planting trees, because balloons become litter | Let them go over the sea, where they will sink and disappear | Use bigger balloons that fly higher | Let them go at night, when all the animals are asleep and safe
test: A girl wants to build a stone tower with rocks from the stream. What is the problem? | She would destroy homes of insects and fish | The tower will fall over in the wind | Stream rocks are too slippery to stack | Nothing at all; streams have plenty of rocks
test: Why do wild lizards usually die when children take them home? | They need their wild home and food to survive | They are allergic to people's hands and skin | Houses are always far too cold for lizards to live in | Lizards die when they see a cage
test: A park ranger in Canada asks visitors to be quiet near a lake. Why? | Noise frightens animals and disturbs other visitors | Loud sound makes the lake water become polluted | Loud voices make it rain | The fish will jump right out of the water if they hear you
test: Which is true about picking up litter you did not drop? | It helps nature and sets a good example for others | It is only the council's job, never yours | You must never touch any litter at all, even with gloves on | It encourages people to drop more litter
test: You see a man pulling a bird's nest out of a hedge. What is the safe and right thing? | Tell a grown-up or a ranger, who can deal with it | Fight him to save the nest and the eggs | Yell rude words at him | Take the nest home with you to keep it safe in your room
test: Why should you never scratch your initials into a tree? | It spoils the place for others and harms the tree | Trees cannot feel anything, so it is always fine | It makes the tree grow faster | Only the very big old trees mind being scratched
test: no bin anywhere, can i just chuck my wrapper in the bush | No, keep it in your bag till you find a bin | Yes, it will rot away quickly in there | Yes, if you hide it well | Only if it's small n nobody sees u
test: apple core's natural, fine to throw it in the grass? | No, take it home or bin it | Yes, it rots in a day | Yes, animals need the food | Only if you throw it far
test: for nan's party can we let the balloons go?? | No, balloons come down as litter that hurts animals | Yes, they just float away up to space | Yes, if they're colourful | Only helium balloons, cos they go higher n never come down
test: caught some tadpoles, gonna keep em in my room | No, look and put them back in their pond | Yes, they love jam jars on a sunny windowsill | Yes, feed them bread | Only for a few weeks till they're frogs
explain: A girl in Florida dropped a plastic bag on the beach; a sea turtle later swallowed one like it, thinking it was a jellyfish, and died.
explain: Children in a park in Ireland left broken glass bottles in the grass; a dog cut its paw badly and a small child cut her foot.
fact: Plastic in the sea kills many sea birds, turtles and fish that eat it or get tangled.
fact: Broken glass left on the ground can cut people and animals for years, so if you break a bottle, tell a grown-up and help clean it up safely.
test: no bin here, just leave the wrapper | Take it home, litter hurts animals | Bury it deep in the sand where it can't blow | Throw it in the river so it floats off | Leave it under a rock where no one sees
test: Why is a plastic bag dangerous for a sea turtle? | It eats it thinking it's a jellyfish | Turtles just don't like plastic | Bags float away | It scares them away from their beaches
test: broke a glass bottle on the path | Tell an adult and help clean it up safely | Leave it, someone will sweep it up later | Kick it into the grass | Pick up every bit with bare hands
test: did u hear a turtle died at the beach and they found it was full of plastic bags inside? real lesson? | Take your litter home; plastic in the sea kills animals | Turtles should just stay away from beaches with people | Only big plastic is a problem | Plastic melts away in the sea anyway, so it doesn't matter
test: apparently at a party they let off 100 balloons and a cow ate one and died. what's the true lesson? | Never release balloons; they come down as litter | Only release a few balloons at a time | Use red balloons, because animals always avoid red | Cows never eat balloons
test: my mate says apple cores are fine to chuck in the bush cos they rot in like a day. right? | No, they take months and draw animals near people | Yes, they vanish overnight, gone by morning | Yes, they feed the trees | Yes, n animals never eat them anyway
exam: Identify the appropriate disposal of a snack wrapper where no bin is nearby. | It is kept in one's pocket or bag until a bin is found | It is dropped in a place where it cannot be seen by others | It is buried beneath the soil | It is hidden deep within a hedge beside the path
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the release of balloons outdoors? | They return as litter swallowed by animals or entangling them | They float away permanently into space | They are entirely harmless once they have burst in the air | They decay within a single day
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of wild frogs and tadpoles. | They are observed but left in their home | They are collected and kept in jars | They are taken home and kept as pets | They are moved to a different pond, where they may be safer
exam: Ascertain the appropriate action upon seeing a person start a fire in the countryside. | To inform an adult, a ranger or the police without arguing | To argue with the person at once | To put out the fire with one's bare hands as fast as possible | To ignore the matter entirely
